# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization, in the repo AND in the working
# tree. `text=auto` alone only normalizes the committed blob and leaves the checked-out
# ending to each machine's `core.autocrlf`, which drifts: git writes CRLF on checkout,
# but every tool that later rewrites a file (sed, esbuild, node scripts, the refresh-data
# Action on Linux, an editor) writes LF, and nothing converts it back. The tree ends up
# half and half. Git's own diff normalizes before comparing so it looks clean, but tools
# that read the working file directly (GitHub Desktop) see a whole-file rewrite and show
# a file as changed with no visible diff. `eol=lf` pins both sides to LF and ends it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell hooks must stay LF — a CR in the shebang breaks /bin/sh
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Apps Script sources round-trip through clasp, which stores LF upstream.
# A CRLF working copy is byte-different from the remote even when the content
# matches, so every `clasp push` stops to ask "Manifest file has been updated.
# Do you want to push and overwrite?" — keep them LF and the prompt goes away.
scripts/sheets/** text eol=lf
